iority inheritance all pass).\n39\t\n40\tTwo minor findings (E020 sizing, E050/E001 gating metadata) and one cosmetic nit; neither rises to\n41\ta rule violation that breaks execution. The DAG is coherent, branch handling is exemplary, and all\n42\trule-mandated acceptance criteria are present.\n43\t\n44\t## Criterion Details\n45\t\n46\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n47\tAll five P0 HLRs map to epics: Konflux/FIPS operator images → E014 (chain E010, E012); DSC\n48\tenable/disable component controller pattern → E020; Removed cleanup → E020 (scope gated by E004);\n49\tupstream-first policy → E010/E011 with sync-workflow ACs (and BRANCH-A-E016 if remediation\n50\ttriggers); TP opt-in default Removed → E020. All three P1 HLRs map (dashboard infrastructure →\n51\tE030, CSV packaging → E021, AuthBridge image → E015 with chain E011, E013). The P2 multi-arch HLR\n52\tis covered as explicit opportunistic ACs in E014/E015 with justification — arm64 is incidental to\n53\tthe P0/P1 build work, not a distinct deferrable feature, so there is no priority collapse.\n54\tPriority inheritance verified edge by edge: E014(P0)←E010(P0), E015(P1)←E011(P1), E020(P0)←E004(P0),\n55\tE030(P1)←E003(P1), E040(P1)←E020(P0)+E021(P1) — no inversion. E050 (docs-authoring, P0) derives\n56\tits priority from the strategy's `priority: Critical` per the docs-authoring exemption; its\n57\tdependencies on P1 implementations are not flagged. E011 (P1) touches the P0 upstream-first HLR\n58\tbut blocks no P0 work. Traceability matrix present in the summary and per-epic HLR sections.\n59\t\n60\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n61\tAll 15 main-plan diagram edges cross-checked against frontmatter `dependencies` in both\n62\tdirections — exact match (E010→E014, E011→E015, E004→E020, E020→E021, E003→E030, E020/E021→E040,\n63\tand all eleven edges into E050). Arrows run dependency→dependent per convention. No cycles.\n64\tImplementation-type parallelism verified exhaustively: the konflux-onboarding epics (E014, E015,\n65\tBRANCH-B-E017) have no `dependencies`/DAG edge to any general implementation epic (E020, E021,\n66\tE030, E040, E050, BRANCH-A-E016) in either direction; the only coupling is the Rule 25\n67\tbuild-pipeline-green and pinned-digest ACs on E020/E021 — correctly done as AC gates, not edges.\n68\tNo license-validation→repo-onboarding/konflux edges (Rules 7-8). Image digests, manifests, and\n69\tRELATED_IMAGE references are consistently treated as configuration references (ACs), not DAG\n70\tedges, including the deliberately-absent E014/E015→E020/E021 and E020→E030 edges, each justified.\n71\tBranch files both set `branch` and `gated_by` matching their filenames and gating Investigations;\n72\tno main-plan epic lists a branch epic in its dependencies. Critical path 5\n73\t(E004→E020→E021→E040→E050) matches the summary and sits within the L heuristic (5-7). The extra\n74\tE001→E050 edge beyond the Rule 11 implementation set is explicitly justified (image-contract\n75\tdocumentation content) and serializes nothing — docs is already last and E001 is sprint-1 work;\n76\tsee Criterion 4 for the associated metadata nit. Cosmetic nit only: the mermaid node label reads\n77\t\"E11\" instead of \"E011\" (node ID and all edges are correct).\n78\t\n79\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (1/2)\n80\tComponent/team tuples are clean throughout: Agentic/Kagenti (E001-E002, E010-E015, both branches),\n81\tAI Core Platform/Platform (E004, E020, E021), AI Core Dashboard/Dashboard (E003, E030), QE/QE\n82\t(E040), Documentation/Docs (E050). No epic spans multiple components or teams. No scope\n83\tduplication: E010/E011 and E012/E013 are per-repo splits of the same workflow (distinct repos,\n84\tdistinct deliverables); E014/E015/BRANCH-B-E017 are per-image splits (distinct Konflux components);\n85\tE050 is pure content authoring and E040 pure QE — no work-product type mixing. One finding: E020\n86\tbundles the entire controller workstream (component CRD, DSC CR schema extension, registry\n87\tregistration, action pipeline, kustomize manifest integration, cleanup-semantics implementation,\n88\tRBAC coexistence verification), which the strategy itself estimates at 2-3 sprints — above the\n89\t~2-week epic guideline even discounting the lifecycle-testing portion now carried by E040. It\n90\tremains a single logical change for a single team following 16 precedents, so the boundary is\n91\tdefensible, but a split (CRD/schema/registry skeleton vs controller pipeline + cleanup) would\n92\timprove independent plannability.\n93\t\n94\t### 4. Type Correctness (1/2)\n95\tAll four Investigations genuinely change downstream structure: E001 can add BRANCH-A-E016 or\n96\tforce TP scope reduction; E002 determines BRANCH-B-E017's existence; E003 rewrites E030's scope;\n97\tE004 rewrites E020's cleanup/finalizer scope. All Implementations produce concrete artifacts\n98\t(decision records, forks, clearance records, pipelines, controllers, CSV, test suites, published\n99\tdocs). Gating metadata is complete and correct on the four gated epics (E020, E030, BRANCH-A-E016,\n100\tBRANCH-B-E017): `gated_by` targets all appear in the direct `dependencies` lists, and\n101\t`gate_failure_impact` carries both `action` and `fallback_approach`. One metadata gap: E050 lists\n102\tInvestigation E001 in `dependencies` with `gated_by: null`. The decomposition documents the edge\n103\tas a content dependency (the image-contract doc is written from the validated contract), which\n104\tkeeps this minor rather than major — the outcome changes the documentation content, not the\n105\tepic's existence or structure — but the gating convention expects either `gated_by` +\n106\t`gate_failure_impact` or an explicit reclassification of the edge.\n107\t\n108\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n109\tType-appropriate signal fields on all 17 epics: the four Investigations carry\n110\t`investigation_signals` (5-signal rubric) with `ai_signals: null`; all thirteen Implementations\n111\t(main-plan and branch)