Science Cluster CR, and the minimal image contract documentation that tells customers how to build a discoverable agent image. This is substantive new content authoring in the Red Hat docs content process, and it runs last so the docs describe what was actually built.\n52\t\n53\t## Scope\n54\t\n55\tContent outline (to be authored inside the Red Hat docs toolchain):\n56\t\n57\t- **Enabling the kagenti-operator (installation):** how to enable the component via the DSC CR (`managementState: Managed`), the Tech Preview opt-in default (`Removed`), what gets deployed (operator, CRDs, RBAC, webhook in `redhat-ods-applications`), and how to disable it (`managementState: Removed`) with the resulting resource cleanup behavior, including the documented behavior of user-created AgentRuntime CRs\n58\t- **Minimal agent image contract:** how to build an agent image any framework can use — the capability description endpoint format (per the validated metadata format the operator consumes), health check expectations, and card structure (endpoint, health check, card fields), based on the upstream validation findings\n59\t- **Onboarding an agent:** creating an AgentRuntime CR that selects an existing Deployment (targetRef selection), and what the platform then does (registration, discoverability, console visibility) at Tech Preview scope\n60\t- **Disconnected installation:** mirroring the kagenti-operator and AuthBridge images for air-gapped clusters via the `RELATED_IMAGE_*` mechanism\n61\t- **Upgrade notes:** TP maturity caveats and the absence of backwards-compatibility concerns (new component, disabled by default)\n62\t\n63\t## Acceptance Criteria\n64\t\n65\t- Installation and enablement documentation covers DSC-based enablement and disablement, including the cleanup behavior on disable\n66\t- The minimal image contract documentation covers endpoint format, health check, and card structure, matching the validated contract the operator enforces\n67\t- Agent onboarding documentation