\tepics (E011, E002, E003, E021) block only P1 work or the docs epic; E050's P0 as a\n72\tdocs-authoring epic correctly derives from the strategy's `priority: Critical` rather than\n73\tpushing P0 upstream. No priority collapse with deferrable features — the P2 multi-arch work\n74\tis incidental polish on the P0/P1 image builds, correctly kept inside the image epics.\n75\t\n76\t### 2. DAG Coherence (0/2)\n77\t\n78\tThe main-plan DAG is well-constructed: every diagram edge matches the target epic's\n79\tfrontmatter `dependencies` and vice versa (verified edge by edge, including all eleven\n80\tRule 11 edges into E050); arrows follow the dependency→dependent convention; Rule 7/8/10\n81\tparallelism is respected (no license-validation→repo/konflux edges, no\n82\tkonflux-onboarding→general-implementation edge — the SHA256 digest and manifest couplings\n83\tare correctly realized as Rule 25 AC gates on E020/E021, not edges); critical path length 5\n84\tis within the L-strategy heuristic (5-7); both branch files set `branch` and `gated_by`;\n85\tno main-plan epic depends on a branch epic. However, the conditional branches have missing\n86\tedges discoverable from epic content:\n87\t\n88\t- **Critical — missing edge E011 → BRANCH-B-E017.** BRANCH-B-E017 is a\n89\t  `konflux-onboarding` epic whose scope builds \"from the `opendatahub-io/kagenti-extensions`\n90\t  midstream fork\" — the artifact E011 exists to establish and pin. Rule 5\n91\t  (repo-onboarding → konflux-onboarding serial) requires this edge; the decomposition itself\n92\t  draws E011 → E015 for exactly this reason, so omitting it on the branch epic is internally\n93\t  inconsistent. The branch epic's only dependency is its gate (E002), which could resolve\n94\t  before the fork exists.\n95\t- **Critical — missing edge E010 → BRANCH-A-E016.** Branch A's scope and acceptance\n96\t  criteria require syncing remediation \"into the `opendatahub-io/kagenti-operator` midstream\n97\t  fork\", rebaselining \"the pinned midstream fork commit\" (both established by E010), and\n98\t  rebuilding \"the affected images\" (via E014's Konflux component). None of E010/E014 appears\n99\t  in its dependencies; its only dependency is E001. The sync/rebaseline/rebuild ACs cannot\n100\t  execute on artifacts that do not yet exist.\n101\t- **Major — E021 consumes E002's outcome ungated.** E021's scope states it will \"add\n102\t  entries for additional AuthBridge variants if the Tech Preview variant set includes\n103\t  them\" — work that hinges on the E002 Investigation outcome (and on branch-B digests) — yet\n104\t  E021 has no dependency on or gate from E002. A main-plan epic performing conditional work\n105\t  driven by an Investigation outcome must either depend on the Investigation or leave that\n106\t  work inside the branch.\n107\t\n108\tAny one of these forces this criterion to 0.\n109\t\n110\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (1/2)\n111\t\n112\tComponent/team tuples are clean: every epic carries exactly one canonical component and one\n113\tteam; the two repos, two license scans, and two Konflux onboardings are correctly separate\n114\tepics; QE and Docs are separate; no scope duplication across siblings (no two epics build\n115\tthe same surface); no work-product type mixing (E050 is pure content authoring, E040 pure\n116\ttest authoring). One finding: **E020 spans the full component-controller workstream —\n117\tcomponent CRD, DSC CR schema extension, registry registration, action pipeline, kustomize\n118\tmanifest integration, cleanup implementation, and RBAC coexistence — which the strategy\n119\testimates at 2-3 sprints even after extracting lifecycle E2E testing to E040.** That exceeds\n120\tthe ~2-week epic guideline. The documented Sizing Justification (single repo, single team,\n121\tsingle logical registration following the 16-precedent pattern, skeleton not independently\n122\tshippable) keeps this short of clearly-oversized bundling, so it is scored as a borderline\n123\tminor rather than a boundary violation — but the epic will likely need the internal\n124\tmilestone tracking the justification itself proposes. Minor issue.\n125\t\n126\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n127\t\n128\tAll four Investigations genuinely resolve uncertainty that changes downstream structure:\n129\tE001 can activate BRANCH-A-E016 or force TP scope reduction; E002 activates/drops\n130\tBRANCH-B-E017; E003 rewrites E030's scope (tab vs standalone section); E004 rewrites E020's\n131\tcleanup/finalizer scope. Each Investigation has downstream dependents. All Implementations\n132\tproduce concrete artifacts (forks, clearance records, images/pipelines, controller code,\n133\tCSV, test suite, published docs). Gating metadata is complete and consistent: every epic\n134\tlisting an Investigation in `dependencies` has `gated_by` set (E020/E004, E030/E003,\n135\tE050/E001, both branches); every `gated_by` target appears in that epic's direct\n136\t`dependencies`; every `gate_failure_impact` populates both `action` and `fallback_approach`\n137\twith real changes on failure. E050's gate on E001 is a content-scoped rewrite gate —\n138\tsomething genuinely changes on failure (the image-contract documentation content), so it is\n139\ta true gate rather than a scheduling dependency.\n140\t\n141\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n142\t\n143\tSignal-field typing is correct everywhere: the four Investigation epics carry\n144\t`investigation_signals` (null `ai_signals`); all Implementation epics, including both\n145\tbranches, carry the 9-signal `ai_signals` (null `investigation_signals`). All 17\n146\tai-signals rationale files exist, and every rationale table matches the frontmatter values\n147\t(cross-checked epic by epic). Signal values are consistent with the strategy content and\n148\tthe rubric's direction: E002/E003 correctly avoid `source_accessibility: 1` for\n149\tpending human decisions (scoring 0 with `human_judgment_required: -2`); E020/E030 carry\n150\t`open_questions: -1` matching their active gates; branch A's negative\n151\tchange_specificity/open_questions/external_dependency values match its unknowable\n152\tgap-driven scope. Architecture-context claims verified against `.context/`:\n153\trhods-operator.md, odh-dashboard.md, and PLATFORM.md exist (supporting\n154\t`architecture_claims: 1` on E014/E015/E020/E021/E030), and no kagenti architecture-context\n155\tfile exists (consistent with `architecture_claims: 0` on the fork/license epics and the\n156\tRule 20 onboarding chain). No arithmetic checked, per instructions.\n157\t\n158\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n159\t\n160\tEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the strategy, including the measurable\n161\tGiven/When/Then ACs carried over verbatim where applicable (E020's deployment and cleanup\n162\tACs, E030's agent-list AC, E021's disconnected-install AC). Rule-mandated ACs are present\n163\tacross the board: feature-flag behavior on E030 (both flag-active and Removed-state hiding),\n164\ttechnical-review-before-publication on E050, green-pipeline + `check-payload` FIPS ACs on\n165\tE014, E015, and BRANCH-B-E017, and the Rule 25 configuration-reference gates on E020/E021\n166\t(pinned digest available, license clearance recorded, manifests at pinned commit). No\n167\tschedule constraints are misfiled as ACs — the Konflux lead-time escalation correctly lives\n168\tin E014/E015 scope and the summary's Health Warnings. No epic body references sibling epics\n169\tby draft ID (verified by scan). One minor: BRANCH-B-E017's description claims it \"adds the\n170\tcorresponding `RELATED_IMAGE_*` entries\", but its scope and ACs stop at publishing pinned\n171\treferences \"for CSV consumption\" — the AC set does not cover the work its own description\n172\tstates.\n173\t\n174\t### 7. Completeness (0/2)\n175\t\n176\tMain-plan scope coverage is thorough: all four strategy workstreams (Konflux onboarding,\n177\tDSC component controller, OLM/CSV, dashboard BFF) plus QE and docs have epics; every\n178\tproposed acceptance criterion is mapped; every strategy open question, assumption, and risk\n179\thas an explicit disposition (the Investigation Handling table is complete); the OTEL\n180\topen-scope note and pending process gates are carried as Health Warnings; the\n181\tdocs-authoring epic exists per `Documentation Support: Yes`; out-of-scope runtime behavior\n182\tis consciously excluded with justification. However, the conditional branch for additional\n183\tAuthBridge variants does not fully cover its stated outcome:\n184\t\n185\t- **Major — the `RELATED_IMAGE_*` CSV entries for additional variants have no correctly\n186\t  owned home in the conditional path.** BRANCH-B-E017's description says it \"adds the\n187\t  corresponding `RELATED_IMAGE_*` entries\", but its scope and acceptance criteria only\n188\t  publish the SHA256-pinned references \"so the corresponding entries can be added to the\n189\t  CSV\" — the entry work is not in the branch's scope or ACs. Meanwhile E021 conditionally\n190\t  claims that work (\"add entries for additional AuthBridge variants if the Tech Preview\n191\t  variant set includes them\") but has no dependency on E002 or the branch (see Criterion 2).\n192\t  Per the branch rules, work that hinges on a branch outcome belongs inside the branch; as\n193\t  written, if the variant-set decision includes envoy/lite, the CSV modification is claimed\n194\t  by two epics and correctly scheduled by neither. The conditional outcome \"additional\n195\t  variants ship in Tech Preview\" is therefore only partially covered (onboarding yes, CSV\n196\t  packaging no).\n197\t\n198\tThis major issue forces the criterion to 0.\n199\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-80d8b2e2dbd4618f","agent_id":"a718a47f9410d0a38","session_id":"e32792c3-9f26-4396-a9d5-fe6ebf0cdb37","uuid":"efb3e5e1-5033-4280-87bd-48cf6adcd104","timestamp":"2026-10-22T05:34:13.051Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-aed040b1c96b7529","permis